   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to authorizing\nadditional supervision and regulation of limited-profit housing companies",
    "basePrintNo": "S7079",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7079-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §32-a, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7079       REVISED 11/04/2021\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to author-\nizing additional supervision and regulation of limited-profit housing\ncompanies\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo mandate and standardize training for Mitchell-Lama shareholders\nelected to serve on the company's board of directors.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends section 32-a of the private housing\nfinance law to direct the commissioner or supervising agency of a limit-\ned-profit housing company to:\n\n*develop and require every voting member of the board of directors of\nsuch a company to complete at least two hours of training on the finan-\ncial oversight, accountability and fiduciary responsibilities of a board\nmember and supplemental training on the powers, functions and duties of\nthe board at least once every three years, and to demonstrate completion\nfor company record; and\n\n*require the board of directors to meet with the commissioner or super-\nvising agency on the financial oversight, accountability and fiduciary\nresponsibilities of the board; the powers, functions and duties of such\na board; and the powers and duties of other governing and administrative\nauthorities affecting limited-profit housing companies.\n\nSection two of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nServing on the board of directors of a residential cooperative is a\ncomplex duty, with elected shareholders exercising broad authority over\nthe financial interests of the company. The board of a limited-profit,\nor Mitchell-Lama, housing company must also navigate the regulatory\nframework under which such companies operate to retain affordability for\ntheir residents.\n\nThis bill would require and standardize, in statute, regular financial\nand regulatory training for Mitchell-Lama shareholders serving on their\ncompany's board of directors, enabling such individuals to more easily\nunderstand and comply with applicable law and to make stronger decisions\nfor the long-term health of their cooperatives.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill in the Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
